      '<strong data-case-part="true" class="text-stone-100">1. Background</strong>',
      "Communism is both a theoretical end-state and a historical political tradition. In classical Marxist theory, communism describes a society in which class divisions and private ownership of productive capital have disappeared and, ultimately, coercive state structures are no longer necessary. Historical governments commonly described as communist did not claim to have reached that final stateless condition. They generally described themselves as socialist transitional states led by communist parties.",
      "This distinction matters, but it cannot be used to separate theory entirely from historical outcomes. Political ideas must be evaluated both by their stated goals and by the institutions repeatedly created when movements attempt to implement them.",
