      '<strong class="text-stone-100">Class Critique</strong>',
      "Communism begins from a real observation: ownership structures affect bargaining power, political influence, and the distribution of gains from production.",
      '<strong class="text-stone-100">Universal Material Security</strong>',
      "A system organized around need rather than purchasing power promises to eliminate extreme deprivation and reduce the insecurity created by unemployment or lack of property.",
      '<strong class="text-stone-100">End of Economic Domination</strong>',
      "If productive assets are collectively owned, no private owner can independently control employment or accumulate unlimited economic power over others.",
      '<strong data-case-part="true" class="text-stone-100">4. Structural Problems</strong>',
      '<strong class="text-stone-100">The Transition Problem</strong>',
      "A stateless end-state is usually approached through a highly empowered transitional state. This creates a paradox: the institution expected eventually to disappear first receives extraordinary authority over property, production, media, political organization, and coercion.",
      '<strong class="text-stone-100">Information Problem</strong>',
      "Large economies contain enormous amounts of local and changing information. Central planners must decide what to produce, how much, where, at what quality, and with which inputs without fully relying on decentralized market prices.",
      '<strong class="text-stone-100">Incentive and Accountability Problem</strong>',
      "Managers and officials still possess personal incentives even when private profit is abolished. Bureaucratic advancement, political loyalty, access, status, and control can replace monetary profit rather than eliminating self-interest.",
      '<strong class="text-stone-100">Exit Problem</strong>',
